WEBVTT

NOTE Web version: M1 narration as subtitles (the synthetic voice is omitted).

00:11.500 --> 00:15.200
The oral accounts of ancient earthquakes
from Chile's native population,

00:15.200 --> 00:18.800
prior to the fifteenth century,

00:19.600 --> 00:23.000
have not survived, with the exception
of the Mapuche culture,

00:23.000 --> 00:27.000
whose worldview, after the creation,

00:27.000 --> 00:32.200
depicted earthquakes as the struggle
between the gods Tren Tren and Caicai Vilu.

00:33.200 --> 00:38.700
Caicai Vilu was a serpent that lived in the ocean
and caused great tsunamis.

00:40.400 --> 00:44.100
Tren Tren protected the Mapuche
by raising the hills.

00:44.800 --> 00:48.700
This legend encouraged the Mapuche people
to climb to the top of the hills

00:48.700 --> 00:52.600
with food whenever an earthquake struck.

00:55.100 --> 01:00.500
The Latin American countries share
a common history linked to the arrival

01:00.500 --> 01:05.700
of the Spanish and Portuguese in the late fifteenth
and early sixteenth centuries.

01:06.700 --> 01:10.900
The Europeans did not preserve the culture
or the history of the native peoples,

01:11.600 --> 01:15.600
so information exists only
from the period of the European conquest,

01:16.200 --> 01:18.700
roughly five centuries.

01:19.200 --> 01:24.500
From the late fifteenth century, information
about earthquakes can be found

01:24.500 --> 01:29.800
in books, newspapers and letters sent to the kings
by the Spanish settlers,

01:29.800 --> 01:34.900
preserved to this day in the
Archivo General de Indias, in Seville, Spain.
